    "name": "1549_B. Gregor and the Pawn Game",
    "description": "Gregor is learning chess, and he has just learned about the pawn piece. He wants to practice moving pawns on a chessboard.\n\nGregor has set up a row of n squares, numbered from 1 to n from left to right. There are two types of pawns: enemy pawns (represented by '1') and Gregor's pawns (represented by '1' as well). Initially, there may be enemy pawns on some squares in the row, and Gregor's pawns are one row below the enemy pawns.\n\nGregor can move each of his pawns in one of the following ways:\n1. One square forward (up) if the square directly above is empty.\n2. One square diagonally (up and left) if there is an enemy pawn on that square.\n3. One square diagonally (up and right) if there is an enemy pawn on that square.\n\nGregor wants to know the maximum number of his pawns that can reach the top row (where the enemy pawns are initially placed) if he moves optimally.\n\nHelp Gregor determine this maximum number.",
    "public_tests": {
        "input": ["4\n3\n010\n111\n4\n0110\n1110\n5\n11111\n11111\n3\n000\n111"],
